UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: This Act, enacted on April 27, 2009, aims to prevent and control domestic violence in Nepal, ensuring every person''s right to a secure and dignified life. It makes domestic violence punishable and provides protection to victims. The Act defines domestic violence, prohibits its commission, and outlines procedures for filing complaints. Courts can grant interim protection orders, hold proceedings in camera, and adopt summary procedures. Perpetrators may bear treatment expenses and pay compensation to victims. The Act also establishes a Service Centre and Service Fund to support victims, sets penalties and a limitation period for filing cases, and clarifies that it does not hinder filing cases under other prevailing laws. It includes provisions for framing rules.

PARTS:
Preamble (Introductory matter) — Explains the purpose of the Act: to protect the right to a secure and dignified life, prevent and control family violence, and punish offenders while protecting victims.
Sections (Sections) — Contains 17 sections covering definitions, prohibition of domestic violence, filing complaints, court actions, interim orders, in-camera proceedings, summary procedures, expenses, compensation, service centres, funds, penalties, limitation, and rule-making power.' WHERE id = 'doc_domestic_violence_offence_and_punishment_act_2066_2009_2066_domes';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Evidence Act, 2031 (1974) is a Nepali statute enacted to amend and consolidate the law of evidence. It defines key terms and establishes rules for which facts require evidence and which do not, including court presumptions. The Act specifies which facts may be taken as evidence, such as admissions, statements by deceased persons, public documents, and expert opinions. It outlines the burden of proof in criminal and civil matters and covers the doctrine of estoppel. The law addresses documentary and oral evidence, including signatures and thumb impressions. It details witness competency, privileges (e.g., spouses, lawyers, public officials), and examination procedures. The Act concludes with provisions on the consequences of improper admission or rejection of evidence and repeals prior laws.

PARTS:
CHAPTER-1 (Preliminary) — Short title, extent, commencement, and definitions.
CHAPTER-2 (Facts requiring or not requiring evidence) — Facts in which evidence may be examined and court presumptions.
CHAPTER-3 (Facts to be taken as evidence) — Admissible facts, including statements, documents, material evidence, and opinions.
CHAPTER-4 (Burden of proof) — Allocation of proof in criminal and civil matters, and for specific facts.
CHAPTER-5 (Estoppel) — The rule preventing a party from denying a previous statement.
CHAPTER-6 (Documentary evidence) — Rules on documents, signatures, and thumb impressions.
CHAPTER-7 (Oral evidence) — Provisions governing the giving of oral testimony.
CHAPTER-8 (Witnesses) — Competency, privileges, and examination procedures for witnesses.
CHAPTER-9 (Miscellaneous) — Consequences of improper evidence admission, saving, and repeal.' WHERE id = 'doc_evidence_act_2031_2031_evidence_act_2031_1974_pdf';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Land Revenue Act, 2034 (1978) establishes the legal framework for land administration and revenue collection in Nepal. It provides for the establishment of Land Revenue Offices, registration of land, and procedures for recovering land revenue, including payment deadlines, fees for late payment, and auction of land for non-payment. The Act also covers exemptions and remissions of land revenue in cases like river cutting or crop failure. It prohibits cultivation or registration of public land and unregistered land without approval. Additional provisions address recovery of arrears from Jimidars (local revenue collectors), punishments for obstruction or unauthorized cultivation, appeals, rule-making powers, and repeals of prior laws. The Act has been amended multiple times, including through the Administration of Justice Act, 2048 (1991).

PARTS:
Chapter-1 (Short Title, Extent and Commencement; Definitions) — Sets the Act''s name, application, and key terms.
Chapter-2 (Establishment of Land Revenue Office; Devolution of Functions) — Creates revenue offices and assigns administrative duties.
Chapter-3 (Registration of Land) — Governs land registration, transmission, and related entries.
Chapter-4 (Power to Recover Land Revenue) — Details payment, recovery, withholding, auction, and receipts.
Chapter-5 (Remission of Land Revenue) — Allows relief for river cutting or crop non-yielding.
Chapter-7 (Prohibition on Registration or Cultivation) — Restricts use of public or unregistered land without approval.
Chapter-8 (Recovery of Arrears; Punishments; Appeal; Rules) — Deals with arrears, penalties, appeals, and implementation powers.' WHERE id = 'doc_land_revenue_act_2034_1978_2034_land_revenue_act_2034_pdf';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Land (Survey and Measurement) Act, 2019 (1963) is a Nepali statute that provides the legal framework for the survey and measurement of land. It establishes the authority and procedures for conducting land surveys, determining land area, demarcating borders and boundaries (including Village Development Committee boundaries), and issuing notices before entering land. The Act also addresses changes in land area, registration certificates, recovery of survey expenses, categorization of land by grade, and maintenance of maps and records. It prescribes penalties for violations, provides a right of appeal, empowers the government to frame rules for implementation, validates previous actions taken under earlier laws, and repeals prior legislation. The Act has been amended multiple times between 1967 and 2010 to update its provisions, including through the Administration of Justice Act, 2048, and the Republic Strengthening and Some Nepal (Amendment) Act, 2066.

PARTS:
### Preamble (Introductory matter) — Provides the title, enactment date, and amendment history of the Act.
### Sections (Sections) — Contains 16 sections covering survey powers, land area determination, boundary demarcation, notices, registration certificates, expense recovery, land grading, maps, penalties, appeals, rule-making, validation, and repeal.' WHERE id = 'doc_land_survey_and_measurement_act_2019_1963_2019_land_survey_and_me';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Right to Information Act, 2064 (2007) is a Nepali statute enacted to make state functions open and transparent, ensuring citizens have easy access to information of public importance held by public bodies. It aims to make public bodies responsible and accountable to citizens while protecting sensitive information that could harm national or citizen interests. The Act establishes procedures for acquiring information, mandates the appointment of Information Officers, and creates a National Information Commission to oversee implementation. It covers classification and protection of information, whistleblower protection, penalties for non-compliance, compensation for damages, and provisions for correcting information. The Act also empowers the government to frame rules for its implementation.

PARTS:
Chapter 1 (Preliminary) — Short title, commencement, and definitions.
Chapter 2 (Right to Information) — Citizen''s right, public body duties, information publication, Information Officer, acquisition procedures, fees, and appeals.
Chapter 3 (Commission) — Establishment, qualifications, tenure, removal, functions, powers, and administration of the Information Commission.
Chapter 4 (Classification and Protection) — Classification of information, protection of sensitive data, whistleblower safeguards, personal information handling, and misuse prohibition.
Chapter 5 (Offenses and Remedies) — Punishments for violations, compensation claims, and appeal mechanisms.
Chapter 6 (Miscellaneous) — Correction of information, good-faith protections, supremacy of this Act, and rule-making power.' WHERE id = 'doc_right_to_information_act_2064_2007_2064_right_to_information_act_';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Act Relating to Children, 2075 (2018) is a Nepali federal law consolidating and amending laws to protect, promote, and fulfill children''s rights, prioritizing their best interests. It defines children and outlines their fundamental rights, including survival, identity, non-discrimination, protection, participation, privacy, health, education, and special rights for disabled children. The Act assigns responsibilities to families, guardians, the State, and media. It establishes a comprehensive juvenile justice system, including special procedures for arrest, investigation, trial, diversion, and Juvenile Courts, along with provisions for child victims, restorative justice, and reform homes. It mandates alternative care and protection for children needing special safeguards, establishes bodies like the National Child Rights Council, details offences against children with punishments and compensation, and includes miscellaneous provisions on duties, confidentiality, age determination, and rule-making powers.

PARTS:
Chapter-1 (Preliminary) — Short title, commencement, and definitions.
Chapter-2 (Rights of the Child) — Enumerates specific fundamental rights of children.
Chapter-3 (Responsibilities) — Duties of families, State, and media towards children.
Chapter-4 (Juvenile Justice) — Procedures for child suspects, trial, Juvenile Courts, and reform.
Chapter-5 (Protection and Alternative Care) — Care, guardianship, children''s homes, and rehabilitation.
Chapter-6 (Institutional Mechanisms) — National and local councils, authority, and child fund.
Chapter-7 (Enforcement) — Roles of local levels in enforcing child rights.
Chapter-8 (Offences and Protection) — Offences against children, rescue, and rehabilitation centres.
Chapter-9 (Punishment and Compensation) — Penalties, compensation, and trial jurisdiction.
Chapter-10 (Miscellaneous) — Duties of child, confidentiality, appeal, age determination, and rules.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_act_relating_to_children_2075_2018_2075_act_relating_to_child';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Consumer Protection Act, 2075 (2018) consolidates Nepali law to protect and promote consumers'' constitutional rights to quality goods and services, provide judicial remedies, and allow compensation for harm. It defines consumers and regulates goods/services quality, labeling, and liability for producers, importers, carriers, hoarders, sellers, and service providers. The Act prohibits unfair trade and activities affecting demand, supply, or price. It establishes oversight bodies like the Consumers Protection Council, Central/Provincial/Local Market Monitoring Committees, and Inspection Officers, with powers of inquiry, inspection, and confiscation. It creates a Consumer Court with jurisdiction, appeal, and execution powers. It also provides for compensation claims, essential goods at fair prices, rewards to informants, and empowers the government to frame rules and directives.

PARTS:
Chapter-1 (Preliminary) — Short title, commencement, and definitions.
Chapter-2 (Consumer Rights and Regulation) — Consumer rights, regulation and quality of goods/services, labeling.
Chapter-3 (Liability) — Liability of producers, importers, carriers, hoarders, sellers, service providers; market levels; returns; invoices.
Chapter-4 (Fair Trade) — Prohibits unfair trade and acts affecting demand, supply, or price.
Chapter-5 (Essential Goods and Pricing) — Lists essential goods/services, price lists, registration, and price standards.
Chapter-6 (Consumers Protection Council) — Formation, meetings, functions, duties, powers of the Council.
Chapter-7 (Market Monitoring) — Central/Provincial/Local committees, teams, inquiry, inspection, testing, confiscation, complaints.
Chapter-8 (Offences and Punishment) — Offences, instant fines, and punishments.
Chapter-9 (Consumer Court) — Constitution, jurisdiction, contempt, appeal, execution, and case procedures.
Chapter-10 (Compensation) — Claiming, providing, and determining standards of compensation.
Chapter-11 (Miscellaneous) — Fair price essentials, support, institutional liability, rewards, prosecution, rules, and saving clause.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_consumer_protection_act_2075_2018_2075_consumer_protection_ac';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 (2008) is a Nepali law enacted on 22 Mansir 2063 (December 8, 2006) to provide legal recognition, validity, integrity, and reliability for electronic records and transactions conducted via electronic data exchange or other electronic communications. It establishes a framework for authenticating digital signatures, securing electronic records, and regulating certifying authorities under a Controller. The Act criminalizes unauthorized access, computer fraud, source-code tampering, and publication of illegal materials electronically, with penalties and tribunals for adjudication. It also governs government use of electronic documents, network service provider liability, and includes provisions for foreign certifying authority recognition, compensation, and rule-making powers.

PARTS:
1–9 (Short Title … Secured Digital Signature) — Definitions, legal recognition of electronic records and digital signatures, and security standards.
10–12 (Electronic Record to be Attributed … Receipt of Electronic Record) — Attribution, acknowledgment, and timing of electronic communications.
13–29 (Appointment of the Controller … Record to be maintained) — Controller’s appointment, powers, licensing of certifying authorities, and oversight.
30–38 (Certifying Authority may issue … Deposit the Private Key) — Certificate issuance, suspension, revocation, and subscriber key obligations.
39–43 (Government Documents may be published … Service Provider not to be Liable) — Electronic government documents and network service provider liability.
44–80 (To Pirate, Destroy or Alter … Inoperativeness of The Electronic Transactions Ordinance) — Offences, penalties, tribunals, appellate procedures, and miscellaneous provisions.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_electronic_transactions_act_2063_2008_2063_electronic_transac';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Labour Act, 2017 (2074) is a comprehensive Nepali statute that consolidates and amends labor laws to define workers'' rights, interests, and benefits. It establishes standards for employment contracts, working hours, remuneration, leave, and social security. The Act prohibits forced labor, child labor, and discrimination, while protecting trade union rights. It outlines dispute resolution mechanisms through Labour Offices, a Labour Court, and collective bargaining processes, including strikes and lock-outs. The law also covers occupational safety, health policies, special provisions for sectors like transport and construction, and penalties for misconduct. It serves as the primary framework for employer-employee relations in Nepal.

PARTS:
1–9 (Preamble … Right to remedy) — Foundational rights, definitions, and prohibitions on exploitation and discrimination.
10–27 (Types of employment … other provisions relating) — Employment contracts, trainee and part-time work, and foreign worker permits.
28–51 (Working hours … matter of right) — Working time, overtime pay, remuneration, and leave entitlements.
52–67 (Contribution for provident fund … collecting fee from labour) — Social security benefits, gratuity, and labor supplier regulations.
68–100 (Formulation of safety … convenient Office) — Workplace safety, health duties, and Labour Office inspection powers.
101–183 (Making application in convenient Office … Repeal and saving) — Councils, dispute resolution, strikes, discipline, Labour Court, and miscellaneous provisions.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_labour_act_2017_2074_2017_the_labor_act_2017_2074_pdf';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Lands Act, 2021 (1964) is a comprehensive Nepali statute that abolishes the Jimidari land revenue system, imposes upper ceilings on land ownership and tenancy, and provides for state acquisition of excess land with compensation. It regulates tenant rights and obligations, sets rent provisions, and mandates compulsory savings and loan recovery processes for tenants. The Act establishes committees and authorities for implementation, grants powers of entry and delegation, and includes miscellaneous provisions on punishment, rule-making, conflict resolution, and repeal. It has been amended multiple times between 1966 and 1999 to address administrative and judicial reforms.

PARTS:
1–6 (Preamble … Punishment) — Citation, definitions, abolition of Jimidari, and revenue collection.
7–12 (Upper ceiling … Exemption from upper ceiling) — Land ownership and tenancy ceilings, restrictions, and exemptions.
13–18 (To submit inventory … Punishment) — Land inventory, acquisition of excess land, and related penalties.
19–24 (Compensation … Punishment) — Compensation for acquired land and restrictions on sale/disposal.
25–32 (Circumstances where tenancy right … Punishment) — Tenant rights, obligations, termination, and records.
33–51 (Provision of rent … Punishment) — Rent, compulsory savings, loan recovery, and tenant protections.
52–64 (Formation of committees … Repeal) — Implementation bodies, appeals, powers, and final provisions.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_lands_act_2021_1964_2021_landact1964_pdf';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Privacy Act, 2075 (2018) is a Nepali federal law enacted to protect the right to privacy of individuals concerning their body, residence, property, documents, data, correspondence, and character. It aims to manage the protection and safe use of personal information held by public bodies or institutions and to prevent encroachment on privacy. The Act defines privacy rights across various domains, including family life, reproductive health, residence, property, documents, data, correspondence, character, and electronic means. It establishes rules for collecting, using, and processing personal information, requires consent for use, and protects sensitive information. It also creates offences, punishments, compensation mechanisms, and related procedural provisions.

PARTS:
1–9 (Chapter 1 … Chapter 9) — Preliminary definitions and substantive privacy rights over one''s person, family, residence, property, documents, data, correspondence, character, and electronic surveillance.
10 (Chapter 10 … Chapter 10) — Rules on collecting, protecting, using, and correcting personal information and sensitive data.
11–13 (Chapter 11 … Chapter 13) — Offences, punishments, complaints, compensation, departmental penalties, consent for minors, and final provisions.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_privacy_act_2075_2018_2075_act_relating_to_individual_privacy';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Social Security Act, 2075 (2018), enacted by Nepal''s Federal Parliament, establishes a legal framework to protect the right to social security for indigent, incapacitated, helpless, disabled, and vulnerable citizens, including helpless single women, children, and tribes on the verge of extinction, as per the Constitution. Chapter 1 provides the short title, commencement, and definitions. Chapter 2 details substantive entitlements, including specific allowances (e.g., senior citizen, destitute, disability, child nutrition) and administrative rules on applications, double benefits, identity cards, distribution, record-keeping, and budget management. Chapter 3 sets out offences, punishments, complaints, trials, and appeals. Chapter 4 covers monitoring, care centers, reports, rule-making powers, directives, schedule alterations, and the Act’s prevailing effect.

PARTS:
Chapter-1 (Preliminary) — Short title, commencement, and definitions.
Chapter-2 (Social Security Entitlements) — Specifies allowances, eligibility, applications, distribution, and records.
Chapter-3 (Offences and Punishment) — Defines offences, complaints, trial, and appeal provisions.
Chapter-4 (Miscellaneous Provisions) — Monitoring, care centers, reports, rule-making, and precedence.' WHERE id = 'doc_the_social_security_act_2075_2018_2075_the_social_security_act_20';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: The Muluki Criminal Code, 2074 (2017) is Nepal''s primary criminal code, establishing general principles of criminal liability, definitions, and application rules. It covers offenses committed abroad, the presumption of innocence, protections against double jeopardy, and exemptions for children under ten and persons lacking mental capacity. The code also addresses lawful acts, mistakes of fact, and the right against self-incrimination. It has been amended by subsequent acts, including the 2079 amendment concerning gender-based violence and money laundering provisions.

PARTS:
Preamble (Introductory matter) — Certification, publication date, and amendment act details.
Sections (Sections) — Definitions, general principles of criminal liability, jurisdiction, and specific provisions on offenses and punishments.' WHERE id = 'muluki-criminal-code-2074';

UPDATE documents SET summary = 'SUMMARY: This is a compilation of amendments to Nepal''s criminal and civil codes, primarily the Muluki Criminal (Procedure) Code, 2074. It includes acts from 2079 and 2080 that modify various provisions related to criminal offenses, procedures, and related laws. The document''s sections cover definitions, the filing of complaints, penalties for failure to report, preservation of evidence, arrest of suspects, preliminary investigations, formation of investigation teams, and restrictions on false or misleading information. It also addresses property laundering (money laundering) and governance-related service provisions. The act provides for separate legal procedures and limits on custody during inquiries, ensuring the efficient handling of criminal cases under the amended framework.

PARTS:
Preamble (Introductory matter) — Declaration and certification of the amendment act, referencing the Muluki Criminal Procedure Code, 2074.
Sections (Sections) — Amendments to various Nepali acts, definitions, complaint filing, investigation procedures, arrest, evidence preservation, and related criminal justice provisions.' WHERE id = 'muluki-criminal-procedure-code-2074';
